These tapes are PrintHive's own captures of real printers, scrubbed before commit.
Each is JSON Lines: a header line, then one line per MQTT frame with `t` (seconds
from capture start), `dir` (`rx` from the printer, `tx` to it), `topic`, and
`frame` (or `raw` for a payload that was not a JSON object).

p1s-01.09.00.00/idle-2026-09-18.jsonl
  A Bambu Lab P1S on firmware 01.09.00.00, idle, with one AMS. Imported from
  PrintHive/hive at 9e767d21b1 (apps/hive-link/src/scripts/mock-printer/tapes/bambu/
  p1s-01.09.00.00/), the commit that scrubbed it, then passed through
  scripts/scrub-tape.ts, which also replaced the print job name and the cloud task id.

Scrubbing replaces device and module serials with <prefix>MOCK<counter>, cloud
task, project and profile ids with small counters, IPv4 addresses with RFC 5737
and IPv6 addresses with RFC 3849 documentation addresses, and URLs with a
placeholder. It blanks MAC addresses, Wi-Fi names, hostnames, device and user
names, credentials, RFID identifiers and job names. Version fields keep their
versions; every other wire value is kept. test/tape-privacy.test.ts checks every tape
here, so a tape that still holds a real identifier fails the build.

Add a tape with: bun scripts/scrub-tape.ts <capture.jsonl> test/fixtures/tapes/<model>-<firmware>/<scenario>-<date>.jsonl
Never commit a capture before scrubbing it, and never name a tape after the printer.
